@@ -29,11 +29,17 @@ per hardware setup.
 
 Monitor configurations are managed by Mutter via the Display panel in Settings,
 which uses a D-Bus API to communicate with Mutter. Each time a new configuration
-is applied and accepted, the user level configuration file is replaced with
-updated content.
+is applied and accepted, Mutter replaces the user level configuration file with
+updated content. If the user level file does not exist and the file in the last
+system configuration directory is writable (or can be created in that
+directory), Mutter writes that system level file instead. The default system
+configuration directory is `/etc/xdg`, so this makes configurations applied by
+an administrator available globally without first creating a per-user override.
 
 Previously defined monitor configurations for hardware state other than the
-current are left intact.
+current are left intact. When writing a system level file, Mutter only replaces
+the monitor `<configuration/>` elements that it manages. Policy, comments and
+other content in the file are preserved.
 
 ## Configuration policy
 
@@ -44,6 +50,12 @@ whether configuration via Settings (i.e. D-Bus) should be allowed.
 The default policy is to prioritize configurations defined in the user level
 configuration file, and to allow configuring via D-Bus.
 
+If a stores policy is present, Mutter also uses its priority order when choosing
+where to save a new configuration. A system store can be selected when the file
+in the last system configuration directory is writable or can be created; if it
+is unavailable, Mutter continues with the next store in the policy. If none of
+the stores in the policy can be used, the configuration is not saved.
+
 Changing the policy is possible by manually adding a `<policy/>` element inside
 the `<monitors version="2"/>` element in the `monitors.xml` file. Note that
 there may only be one `<policy/>` element in each configuration file.
